' Runtime test for bug-546: a user-declared `RESOURCE T CLOSE BY op
' THREAD_SENDABLE` crosses a thread boundary, and the handle the receiver
' accepts is the SAME open native resource the sender opened.
'
' Before the fix the program did not build at all:
'
'   error: native inlined field size not available for type 'Db' while lowering
'   bind d AS Db
'
' No code, no file, no line. The cause was not the message's own lookup: codegen
' classifies a value with `flatness_walk`, whose resource arm asked
' `builtins::is_resource_type` — the BUILT-IN registry. A user-declared `Db`
' matched no arm there and fell through to `record_field_is_pointer`, which has
' no resource arm either, so the walk answered `true` for both of its modes. The
' memcpy half is what produced the message (a bare `Db` bind was routed to
' `copy_flat_block`, which then wanted an inlined field size for a type that is
' really an 8-byte handle). The arena-transfer half never got the chance to do
' its damage, and is the worse of the two: it is the predicate that says a thread
' transfer may relocate a block wholesale, and a relocated resource handle points
' into the *sender's* arena, which is per-thread.
'
' WHAT EACH ASSERTION HERE IS FOR.
'
' `used=2000` is an IDENTITY proof, not just a liveness one. Main creates the
' `seeded` table on each database BEFORE transferring it; the worker's only
' statement is an INSERT INTO that same table. A handle that arrived duplicated,
' re-opened, or pointing at a stale block would not have the table, sqlite3_exec
' would return non-zero, `SUCCESS_ON status = 0` would raise, and the worker
' would die with `used` short of 2000. So this counts accepted handles that are
' demonstrably the sender's own open database.
'
' `movedCount=2000` is the sender half of closed-exactly-once. After a successful
' `thread::transfer` the sender's binding is flagged moved, so `sql::exec(db, …)`
' raises ErrResourceMoved (7-703-0009) — and a moved binding is not closed by the
' sender's scope drop either. The receiver's own scope drop then runs the
' registered close op through the path `native-resource-scope-drop-rt` pins. One
' owner at a time, one close.
'
' WHY 2000 ITERATIONS AND NOT 3. The correct arm is the deep copy into the
' RECEIVER's arena (`copy_resource_to_current_arena`); the other candidate
' considered for this bug was the move-only pointer arm, which is layout-agnostic
' and passes a three-iteration smoke test. It is wrong, and the loop is what
' proves it: with the pointer arm the sender's arena is reused underneath the
' receiver and this program dies with 7-705-0009. Do NOT lower the iteration
' count — at low counts both arms look identical.
IMPORT io
IMPORT thread

RESOURCE Db CLOSE BY sql::close THREAD_SENDABLE

LINK "sqlite3" AS sql
  FUNC open(path AS String) AS RES Db
    SYMBOL "sqlite3_open"
    ABI (path CString, db OUT CPtr) AS status CInt32
    RETURN db
    SUCCESS_ON status = 0
  END FUNC
  FUNC exec(RES db AS Db, statement AS String) AS Nothing
    SYMBOL "sqlite3_exec"
    ABI (db CPtr, statement CString, callback CPtr, arg CPtr, errmsg CPtr) AS status CInt32
    CONST callback = NOTHING
    CONST arg = NOTHING
    CONST errmsg = NOTHING
    SUCCESS_ON status = 0
  END FUNC
  FUNC close(RES db AS Db) AS Nothing
    SYMBOL "sqlite3_close"
    ABI (db CPtr) AS status CInt32
    SUCCESS_ON status = 0
  END FUNC
END LINK

' The receiving end. `thread::accept` yields the bare declared type — the exact
' bind (`bind d AS Db`) the pre-fix compiler refused to lower. Each accepted
' handle leaves scope at the end of the iteration, so the registered close op
' runs 2000 times on this thread.
ISOLATED FUNC drain(t AS ThreadWorker OF RES Db TO Integer, rounds AS Integer) AS Integer
  MUT used AS Integer = 0
  MUT i AS Integer = 0
  WHILE i < rounds
    RES d AS Db = thread::accept(t, 5000)
    sql::exec(d, "INSERT INTO seeded VALUES (2);")
    used = used + 1
    i = i + 1
  END WHILE
  RETURN used
END FUNC

FUNC main AS Integer
  LET t AS Thread OF RES Db TO Integer = thread::start(drain, 2000)
  MUT moved AS Integer = 0
  MUT i AS Integer = 0
  WHILE i < 2000
    RES db AS Db = sql::open(":memory:")
    sql::exec(db, "CREATE TABLE seeded(a INTEGER);")
    thread::transfer(t, db, 5000)
    sql::exec(db, "INSERT INTO seeded VALUES (3);") TRAP(err)
      IF err.code <> 77030009 THEN FAIL err
      moved = moved + 1
      RECOVER
    END TRAP
    i = i + 1
  END WHILE
  io::print("used=" & toString(thread::waitFor(t)))
  io::print("movedCount=" & toString(moved))
  RETURN 0
END FUNC
